  "worked_solution": "First, we graph the function $g(x)=2x-6.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14209261/assets/de124058817fded27ad4ca9f9537f4a7d09ccd8836c96b0e64580e28dc9ab1ec.png)\n\nThen, we find the lowest and highest $y$-values in the graph over the domain $x\\in [2,4].$\n\n* The lowest $y$ value is $-2.$ This occurs at the point $(2,-2).$\n\n* The highest $y$ value is $2.$ This occurs at the point $(4,2).$\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-2$ and $2,$ including the endpoints.\n\nTherefore, the range is $-2\\le y\\le 2,$ which can also be written as \n\n$$\n-2\\le g(x)\\le 2.\n$$",
